$ item contract static
2:description: Verify one issue acceptance criterion against implementation evidence with adversarial false-green checks
24:This is a read-only verification goal. Do not modify repository files, stage files, commit, push, publish, or remediate implementation gaps. Your job is to determine whether this one criterion is truly satisfied and to produce a structured result that the acceptance pipeline can aggregate.
28:Use this embedded context as a starting point, then inspect files directly before deciding. If `item_context_status` is `unresolved`, stop the item with status `blocked` and explain the blocker.
88:- desired behavior;
90:- implementation checklist or closeout notes;
91:- proof threat model;
92:- required proofs;
95:Restate the user-visible invariant implied by this criterion in one concise sentence. Do not treat this restatement as proof.
97:### 2. Map criterion to implementation and proof surfaces
99:Identify what files, docs, probes, commands, or live behavior should demonstrate this criterion. Use targeted inspection rather than broad wandering.
104:- `git diff -- <relevant path>` for uncommitted implementation work;
105:- `git log --oneline --decorate -- <relevant path>` when the implementation is committed;
106:- `rg` for feature names, command names, template names, proof ids, or status strings;
108:- validation logs or probe files named in `required_proofs[]`.
110:Run targeted deterministic or live checks only when they are non-destructive, proportionate, and relevant to this specific criterion. Do not run broad expensive gates unless this criterion depends on them or the issue proof contract requires them.
112:### 3. Attack false-green paths
114:Before deciding `green`, actively check for false-green risks:
117:- The implementation exists but is not discoverable through the intended template/tool path.
118:- A proof command exists but validates a weaker behavior than the criterion.
119:- A validation artifact is stale or predates the relevant implementation.
125:If a false-green risk is plausible and you cannot rule it out with available read-only evidence, return `red` or `blocked`, not `green`.
131:- `green`: the criterion is independently verified with concrete evidence that would likely fail if the criterion were false.
132:- `red`: the criterion is not met, is contradicted by evidence, or has only weak/vague evidence when stronger evidence is available.
133:- `blocked`: verification cannot be completed because required authority, environment, credentials, live service, or safety permission is missing.
138:- `medium`: strong static evidence exists, but a live or broader proof would improve confidence.
139:- `low`: evidence is suggestive only; prefer `red` or `blocked` unless the criterion itself is documentation-only.
